<Project>

    <!--
        TUnit.Assertions adds a reference to TUnit.Assertions.FSharp, at a floating version, to every
        F# project that uses it, from a .props file inside the package. Restore never sees that
        reference, because it evaluates with package imports switched off, but an IDE's evaluation
        does - so an IDE restore writes it into the lock file, and CI's locked restore then fails. The
        note on EnableTUnitPolyfills in Directory.Build.props covers the same problem for Polyfill.

        That one has a switch; this one does not, so it is removed. It has to happen here rather than
        in Directory.Build.props: a package's .props file is imported after that file, so a Remove
        there would run before the reference existed. Nothing depends on the package - CI has never
        restored it.
    -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectExtension)' == '.fsproj'">
        <PackageReference Remove="TUnit.Assertions.FSharp"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        The loop analyzer runs on every C# project here, so that the solution's own code is held to
        the rules a consumer's is. It is a project reference whose output goes to the compiler as an
        analyzer and not as a reference: OutputItemType="Analyzer" puts the built dll in the Analyzer
        items, the way a consumer's package does, and ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" keeps it out of
        the compile references, the output folders, and the restore graph, so no lock file carries
        it. PrivateAssets="all" keeps it out of the dependencies of every package. It is here and not
        in each project for the reason EnableDefaultItems is in Directory.Build.props: a reference
        every project has to remember is one a new project forgets, and a project without the
        analyzer would be the one that reads a loop early without hearing about it.

        The analyzer reaches a consumer inside the SodaFlow package, under analyzers/dotnet/cs, which
        SodaFlow.csproj packs from the analyzer's build output; see the note there. It is not a
        package of its own: NuGet leaves a ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" reference out of the
        restore graph that pack reads its dependencies from, so SodaFlow could not depend on a
        sibling analyzer package, and the spelling that keeps the reference in the graph also makes
        the dll a compile reference of every project, which conflicts with the compiler's own
        assemblies. Both were tried and read back from the nuspec and the reference items.

        Two kinds of project are left out. The analyzer and its tests do not analyze themselves; the
        tests run the analyzer in process over a shapes file that is meant to trip every rule, and a
        build of that file under the analyzer would fail. F# projects are left out because a Roslyn
        analyzer runs in the C# and VB compilers only; the F# surface keeps the runtime check, as
        the loops page says.
    -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectExtension)' == '.csproj' and !$(MSBuildProjectName.StartsWith('SodaFlow.Analyzers'))">
        <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Analyzers\SodaFlow.Analyzers\SodaFlow.Analyzers.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                          PrivateAssets="all"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
